Networking topic :- Difference between TCP and UPD. Give example where each is used.

TCP (Transmission Control Protocol)
	1. Connection-oriented (handshake before communication).
	2. Reliable – ensures all packets are delivered, ordered, and retransmitted if lost.
	3. Slower than UDP due to error checking and reliability.
	4. Use cases: SSH, HTTP/HTTPS, FTP, Email (SMTP/IMAP/POP3).

UDP (User Datagram Protocol)
	1. Connectionless (no handshake, just sends packets).
	2. Unreliable – packets may be lost or arrive out of order, but faster due to no overhead.
	3. Lightweight and low latency.
	4. Use cases: DNS queries, video/audio streaming, VoIP, gaming, monitoring tools (SNMP).

In short , TCP is connection-oriented and reliable but slower — used for things like SSH, web browsing, and email.

Whereas UDP is connectionless and faster but less reliable — used for DNS, streaming, gaming, and VoIP.
